---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '@/lib/data';
---

<BaseLayout title="Dataset quality · NameFrame" description="How to interpret NameFrame sample grades, visible failures and known limitations.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={withBase('/')}>Home</a><span>/</span><span>Quality</span></nav>
    <p class="kicker">Evidence, not absolutes</p>
    <h1>A grade is a summary—not a guarantee.</h1>
    <p class="lede">The useful part of a dataset report is the trail from a check to a finding, a preview and a known limitation.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="quality-grid">
      <article class="panel"><h3>Integrity</h3><p>Can the package be opened, matched to its declared dimensions and checked against its published SHA-256 digest?</p></article>
      <article class="panel"><h3>Annotation evidence</h3><p>Do labelled objects align with the visual outputs, and are missed or questionable cases made inspectable?</p></article>
      <article class="panel"><h3>Distribution</h3><p>Do classes, viewpoints and splits reveal imbalance, near-duplicates or coverage that is narrower than the intended use?</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="content-grid">
      <article class="prose">
        <h2>How to read a published grade</h2>
        <p>A reported score describes the checks run for that source capture. It does not establish fitness for your model, domain or benchmark. Compare the score with the exact sample version and the limitations beside it.</p>
        <h2>Why failures stay visible</h2>
        <p>The Airbase sample retains a verification finding: three visible people had no published bounding box among ninety inspected. Hiding that case would make the grade less informative.</p>
        <p class="callout">NameFrame does not describe sample labels as “perfect ground truth.” Engine-derived annotations can still encounter visibility thresholds, ID-buffer neighbourhoods, filtering decisions and packaging errors.</p>
        <h2>Before model training</h2>
        <ol><li>Verify hashes and package structure.</li><li>Inspect multiple modalities at object boundaries.</li><li>Measure class and view distributions.</li><li>Check split similarity and duplicate leakage.</li><li>Decide whether the intended domain matches your own.</li></ol>
      </article>
      <aside class="panel side-facts"><p class="status grade-a">Airbase · A · 92.4</p><p>High reported score with a visible missed-box finding and narrow authored camera coverage.</p><p class="status grade-c">Barnyard · C · 78.3</p><p>Penalised for duplicate leakage and class imbalance; its configured archive is also pending publication.</p></aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
